"Thermodynamics of the Arginine Kinase Reaction - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021925818815630)\nciteturn146search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.2 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; The effect of temperature, pH, free [Mg^{2+}], and ionic strength on the apparent equilibrium constant of arginine kinase (EC 2.7.3.3) was determined. ... The K′ at pH 7.0, 1.0 m m free [Mg^{2+}], and 0.25 m ionic strength was 29.91 ± 0.59, 33.44 ± 0.46, 35.44 ± 0.71, 39.64 ± 0.74, and 45.19 ± 0.65 (n = 8) at 40, 33, 25, 15, and 5 °C, respectively. ... In addition, the AK biochemical reaction releases −3.74 kJ mol^{−1} less heat/mol of ATP formed than the CK biochemical reaction.\n\nJournal of Biological Chemistry\n\nVolume 274, Issue 32, 6 August 1999, Pages 22459-22463\n\n# \n\nCELL BIOLOGY AND METABOLISM\n\nThermodynamics of the Arginine Kinase Reaction*\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.274.32.22459Get rights and content\n\nUnder a Creative Commons license\n\nOpen access\n\nThe effect of temperature, pH, free [Mg^{2+}], and ionic strength on the apparent equilibrium constant of arginine kinase (EC 2.7.3.3) was determined. At equilibrium, the apparent K′ was defined as Equation 1${\\mathbf{K}}^{\\mathbf{\\prime }}\\mathbf{=}\\frac{[\\mathbf{\\text{ATP}}][\\mathbf{\\text{Arg}}]}{[\\mathbf{\\text{ADP}}][\\mathbf{\\text{PArg}}]}$where each reactant represents the sum of all the ionic and metal complex species. The K′ at pH 7.0, 1.0 m m free [Mg^{2+}], and 0.25 m ionic strength was 29.91 ± 0.59, 33.44 ± 0.46, 35.44 ± 0.71, 39.64 ± 0.74, and 45.19 ± 0.65 (n = 8) at 40, 33, 25, 15, and 5 °C, respectively. Six years later, Lohmann demonstrated the reaction in muscle extracts prepared from crabs. The first equilibrium constant was performed by Lehmann in 1936, who reported a value of K′_{AK} = 3.1 at pH 7.7 in the direction of ATP, a value close to unity (6). The next major study on the thermodynamics AK was by Szorenyi et al. (7), who reported a constant of 500 at pH 6.1 and 5.9 at pH 9.1. The soviet study provided the first glimpse into the major effect of pH on K′_{AK} (8).\n\nNearly two decades later Uhr et al. (9) studied the effect of magnesium on the AK equilibrium. By substituting the initial rates of reaction into the Haldane relationship, they calculated K′_{AK} = 2.4 at pH 8.0, [Mg^{2+}] = 1.0 m m at 30 °C, and unspecified ionic strength (I) (9). Using a second approach of attaining equilibria, then stopping the reaction and measuring ATP, they obtained K′_{AK} values of 0.82, 0.47, and 0.41 at [Mg^{2+}] concentrations of 0.12, 0.61, and 1.93 m m, respectively (9). In 1969, Smith and Morrison (10) reinvestigated the kinetics and equilibria of arginine kinase and reported a similar value of K′_{AK}= 1.3 in the direction of ATP formation.\n\nIn 1986 Graham et al. (11) measured the forward and reverse fluxes of the arginine kinase using saturation transfer ^{31}P NMR in the isolated scallop adductor muscle in vitro. In addition, these researchers made some equilibrium measurements and reported a value for K′_{AK} of 54 ± 6 at pH 7.1, 25 °C. More recently, Ellington (12) used^{31}P NMR and a multiple equilibria approach to determine the value of K′_{AK} at pH 7.25, 35 °C at unspecified free [Mg^{2+}] and I. By allowing the creatine kinase and the arginine kinase reactions to reach equilibrium simultaneously in solution and assuming a K′_{CK} of 100, these workers calculated that K′_{AK} was 13% that of the value of K′_{CK}, or K′_{AK} = 13 (12). Their NMR value of 13 agreed within 5% of the value determined by more conventional enzymatic analysis (12).\n\nWhat emerges from this brief history is that even the best attempts to estimate K′_{AK} suffer from a number of analytical problems that make it difficult to compare values between laboratories. The aim of the present study is to provide a quantitative assessment of the thermodynamics of the AK reaction at specified conditions of pH, pMg, T, and I. In addition we will examine the effect of temperature (5 to 40 °C), pH (6.4–8.2), free [Mg^{2+}] (0.1 to 4 m m), and ionic strength (0.13–0.34 m). Last, our data will be compared and discussed with the thermodynamic properties of the creatine kinase reaction (13, 14).\n\nAll other chemicals were reagent grade. Phosphoarginine disodium salt, arginine HCl (ultra >99%), phosphate sodium salt (monobasic and dibasic), potassium chloride, magnesium chloride (anhydrous), and the potassium salts of ATP and ADP were purchased from Sigma.\n\n### Instrumentation and Equilibrium Determinations\n\nNADH or NADP oxidation-reduction was monitored at 340 nm and 1-cm path-length using a GBC 918 UV-visible spectrophotometer (GBC Scientific Equipment Pty. Ltd. Melbourne Australia), taking the extinction coefficient of NADH under these conditions to be 6.22 × 10^{+6}cm^{2}/mol at 340 nm. In those instances where the limitations of the UV-visible spectrophotometer were reached (e.g. ADP) when the equilibrium was approached, favoring ATP and arginine formation, the oxidation of NADH was monitored by a change in fluorescence using a Farrand Ratio-2 fluorometer (Optical Technology Devices, Inc., Elmsford, NY) and disposable borosilicate Kimble glass culture tubes (10 × 75 mm).\nThis 1-ml aliquot of reaction buffer was placed in a Centricon 30 spin filter housed in a centrifuge rotor maintained at 5 °C. The sample was then centrifuged at 4500 × g in a centrifuge maintained at the same temperature for 5 min, and the filtrate was retained. The enzyme-free filtrate containing the reactants of the AK reaction were removed and kept at −80 °C until analysis.\n\nThe temperature was then reset on the water bath to 15 °C, new filters were placed in the rotor head, and the reaction was allowed 1 h to establish equilibrium at the new temperature. The same process was repeated at 15, 25, 33, and 40 °C, and the rotor head and centrifuge were maintained at the different temperatures. Experiments studying the variation of pH, free [Mg^{2+}], and I were carried out in a similar manner. For the pH experiment, initial reaction buffers were the same as for the temperature experiment, except the initial pH was adjusted to pH = 6.4, and the initial temperature was set at 25 °C. Potassium hydroxide (10 m stock) was added in μl amounts to adjust the pH to 6.7, 7.0, 7.4, 7.8, and 8.2. For the free [Mg^{2+}] experiment, initial reactions contained only 1.4 m m MgCl_{2}, pH 7.0, and 25 °C. Solid MgCl_{2}·6H_{2}0 was added to adjust free [Mg^{2+}] concentrations from 0.1 to 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, and 4.0 m m. For the ionic strength experiment, initial reactions contained only 10 m m KCl, pH 7.0, and 25 °C. Solid KCl was added to adjust ionic strength from 0.13 to 0.19, 0.24, 0.29, and 0.34 m.\n\n## RESULTS\n\nEquilibrium was judged complete when the apparent K′, [ATP][Arg]/[ADP][PArg], agreed to within 15% at experimental conditions of pH, free [Mg^{2+}], and ionic strength from both directions. Table I shows the concentrations of metabolites before the addition of AK and at the first experimental point taken after 1 h at 5 °C. The results show a mean K′_{AK} of 33.8 in the direction of ATP formation and 38.4 in the opposite direction. Importantly, although this difference is statistically significant (p < 0.05), the data refers to experimental conditions of approximately pH 7.0, a free [Mg^{2+}] of approximately 1.0 m m, and an ionic strength of approximately 0.25 m. When these K′_{experimental} values are adjusted to specified conditions of pH 7.0, free [Mg^{2+}] of 1.0 m m, and ionic strength of 0.25 m, the statistical significance disappears. At these ionic conditions, using our mathematical model we calculate a mean K′_{AK} in the direction of ATP formation of 44.8 ± 0.83 (S.E.) (n = 4) and 45.6 ± 1.07 (n = 4) in the opposite direction. Thus, a 15% criteria for K′_{experimental} satisfies the condition of attainment of equilibrium under our experimental conditions. For the other temperatures, K′_{AK} values at pH = 7.0, free [Mg^{2+}] = 1.0 m m, I = 0.25 m in the forward and reverse directions were as follows: 39.8 ± 1.50 (n = 4) versus39.4 ± 0.54 (n = 4) at 15 °C; 34.6 ± 1.08 versus 36.3 ± 0.84 at 25 °C; 33.9 ± 0.41versus 32.9 ± 0.80 at 33 °C, and 29.2 ± 0.78versus 30.7 ± 0.81 at 40 °C. At all temperatures, K′_{AK} in both directions were not significantly different (p < 0.05). The same was also found for K′_{AK} versuspH, free [Mg^{2+}] and I (Table I). From this point we will only refer to the calculated K′_{AK}at specified conditions.\n\nTable I. Attainment of equilibrium from the forward and reverse directions for the AK reaction\n\nEmpty Cell  | Initial concentrations  | Final concentrations\n[ADP]  | [PArg]  | [ATP]  | [Arg]  | [ADP]  | [PArg]  | [ATP]  | [Arg]  | pH  | [Mg]  | K′_{AK}\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n10 ^{−3}m  | 10 ^{−3}m\nForward direction: PArg + ADP = Arg + ATP\nA I  | 4.60  | 5.58  | 0  | 0  | 0.750  | 0.737  | 3.87  | 4.78  | 7.158  | 5.59  | 33.5\nA II  | 4.60  | 5.58  | 0  | 0  | 0.699  | 0.779  | 3.90  | 4.72  | 7.148  | 5.59  | 33.8\nA III  | 4.60  | 5.58  | 0  | 0  | 0.715  | 0.799  | 3.83  | 4.86  | 7.145  | 5.59  | 32.6\nA IV  | 4.60  | 5.58  | 0  | 0  | 0.713  | 0.747  | 3.89  | 4.81  | 7.146  | 5.59  | 35.1\nReverse direction: Arg + ATP = PArg + ADP\nB I  | 0  | 0  | 4.55  | 6.34  | 0.675  | 0.781  | 3.83  | 5.52  | 7.115  | 5.70  | 40.1\nB II  | 0  | 0  | 4.55  | 6.34  | 0.701  | 0.755  | 3.86  | 5.06  | 7.110  | 5.70  | 36.9\nB III  | 0  | 0  | 4.55  | 6.34  | 0.709  | 0.754  | 3.91  | 5.03  | 7.108  | 5.70  | 36.8\nB IV  | 0  | 0  | 4.55  | 6.34  | 0.673  | 0.727  | 3.92  | 4.93  | 7.107  | 5.70  | 39.6\n\n\nImage\n\n  1. Download: Download full-size image\n\nFigure 1. van't Hoff plot of the effect of temperature on the apparent equilibrium constant of the arginine kinase ( K′ _{AK} ).Each value represents the mean ±S.E. (n = 8) at pH 7.0, free [Mg^{2+}] of 1.0 m m and ionic strength of 0.25 m. Each point represents the mean ±S.E. (n = 8) at 5, 15, 25, 33, and 40 °C. The equation to the line was y = 428 + 0.116R ^{2} = 0.99. The standard apparent heat of the reaction, ΔH°′, was calculated from the slope of the line (slope = −ΔH°′/2.303 R, whereR is the gas constant and equal to 8.314 J mol^{−1}K^{−1}). The intercept of log K′versus 1/T at 1/T = 0 is equal to ΔS°′/2.303 R, where ΔS°′ is defined as the standard apparent entropy of the reaction under the conditions defined. The standard apparent ΔH°′ for the arginine kinase reaction in the direction of ATP formation was −8.19 kJ mol^{−1} and ΔS°′ = +2.22 J K^{−1}mol^{−1}.\n\nTable II. Values of K′_{AK} at varying conditions of temperature, pH, and free [Mg ^{2+}]\n\nTemperature pH 7.0, [Mg^{2+}] = 1.0 m m,I = 0.25 m  | pH T = 25 °C, [Mg^{2+}] = 1.0 m m, I = 0.25 m  | Free [Mg^{2+}] T = 25 °C, pH 7.0, I = 0.25 m\n--- | --- | ---\n |  | 1.0 m m\n45.19 ± 0.65  | 97.02 ± 3.95  | 11.08 ± 0.11\n(5 °C)  | (pH 6.4)  | ([Mg^{2+}] = 0.1 m m)\n39.64 ± 0.74  | 57.79 ± 2.95  | 16.50 ± 0.31\n(15 °C)  | (pH 6.7)  | ([Mg^{2+}] = 0.25 m m)\n35.44 ± 0.71  | 31.11 ± 1.00  | 22.49 ± 0.42\n(25 °C)  | (pH 7.0)  | ([Mg^{2+}] = 0.5 m m)\n33.44 ± 0.46  | 13.80 ± 0.39  | 32.57 ± 2.57\n(33 °C)  | (pH 7.4)  | ([Mg^{2+}] = 1.0 m m)\n29.91 ± 0.59  | 5.74 ± 0.20  | 39.76 ± 1.16\n(40 °C)  | (pH 7.8)  | ([Mg^{2+}] = 2.0 m m)\n | 2.15 ± 0.11  | 46.50 ± 1.07\n | (pH 8.2)  | ([Mg^{2+}] = 4.0 m m)\n\nThe effect of pH on the K′_{AK} is shown in Fig. 2. The AK reaction was studied at six different pH values, 6.4, 6.7, 7.0, 7.4, 7.8, and 8.2 at free [Mg^{2+}] of 1.0 m m, 25 °C, andI = 0.25 m. Like most other kinases, the AK reaction is extremely sensitive to pH. ## DISCUSSION\n\nThis study examined in a quantitative manner the effect of temperature, pH, pMg, and I on the arginine kinase reaction. Table III summarizes the thermodynamics of the AK reaction and compares these values with the CK biochemical reaction at pH 7.0, [Mg^{2+}] =1.0 m m, I = 0.25 m, 25 °C (14). The transformed Gibbs energy (Δ G°′) for the AK reaction is −8.89 kJ mol^{−1} compared with −13.31 kJ mol^{−1} for the CK reaction. In addition, the AK biochemical reaction releases −3.74 kJ mol^{−1} less heat/mol of ATP formed than the CK biochemical reaction. Like CK, however, the ΔG°′_{AK} is comprised mostly of enthalpy (92%) (Table III). The physiological significance of a lower ΔG°′ is that AK, which appears exclusively in invertebrate tissues, supports lower cytosolic [ATP]/[ADP] [P_{i}] compared with CK in vertebrate tissues (12). The underlying assumption for this prediction is that the arginine kinase operates near equilibrium in vivo as has been demonstrated for creatine kinase (19).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Thermodynamics of the Arginine Kinase Reaction (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12870567_Thermodynamics_of_the_Arginine_Kinase_Reaction)\nciteturn146search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Thermodynamics of the Arginine Kinase Reaction* ... strength on the apparent equilibrium constant of argi-nine kinase (EC 2.7.3.3) was determined. ... 0.46, 35.44 6 0.71, 39.64 6 0.74, and 45.19 6 0.65 (n 5 8) at\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nThermodynamics of the Arginine Kinase Reaction*\n\n(Received for publication, February 19, 1999, and in revised form, March 27, 1999)\n\nWalter E. Teague, Jr. and Geoffrey P. Dobson‡\n\nFrom the Department of Physiology and Pharmacology, James Cook University, Townsville, Queensland, Australia 4811\n\nThe effect of temperature, pH, free [Mg\n\n2 1\n\n], and ionic\n\nstrength on the apparent equilibrium constant of argi-\n\nnine kinase (EC 2.7.3.3) was determined. At equilibrium,\n\nthe apparent K*was defined as\n\nK*5[ATP][Arg]\n\n[ADP][PArg] (Eq. 1)\n\nwhere each reactant represents the sum of all the ionic\n\